{
  "_readme": "EMC x TSC1/TSC2, ROUND 5, 2026-08-24 -- closing the two limitations the round-4 measurement named. (1) THE SINGLE-COPY QUESTION, which is the largest gap between what was measured and what the index case reported. Round 4's copy-number payload contained only -2 (homozygous deletion) and 2 (amplification), so a single-copy TSC2 loss would not have been counted -- and 'TSC2 gene loss' on a vendor assay may well BE a single-copy loss. cBioPortal's discreteCopyNumberEventType accepts HETLOSS, so the -1 state is directly requestable. Requested here for TSC1 and TSC2. NOTE: round 4 established that this endpoint IGNORES its entrezGeneId parameter and returns the whole profile, so these payloads must be filtered locally on entrezGeneId; a caller that trusted the parameter would count every gene's losses as TSC's. (2) INDEPENDENCE. The 75 EMC samples came from one institution on one assay, and the only published EMC secondary-alteration series is probably inside them. pan_origimed_2020 (China Pan-cancer, OrigiMed, 10,194 samples) and sarcoma_ucla_2024 are non-MSK and would be genuinely independent. The MSK cohorts below are included to enlarge the count, NOT as independent replication: sarcoma_msk_2022 uses anonymised study-prefixed sample ids while msk_impact_50k_2026 uses real P-ids, so the two CANNOT be deduplicated against each other and their EMC counts must never be added.",
